WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.540
Even good Hollywood movies can be hard to
watch twice.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:08.040
In the case of these films, it's not bad writing
or uninspired performances that make the movies

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.520
hard to screen a second time.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.220
Rather, they're so depressing, shocking, or
even terrifying that one cinematic exposure

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.960
to them is more than enough to last a lifetime.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.660
In the case of these films, they're worth
watching — but you probably won't want to

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.689
see them again after that.

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:24.550
Requiem For a Dream

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:29.259
This adaptation of Hubert Selby's novel is
a brutal, bleak, and visceral film in which

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:34.649
Darren Aronofsky opens a window on the lives
and experiences of four different drug addicts.

00:00:34.649 --> 00:00:36.399
And that view is decidedly grim.

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:37.399
"I like thinking about the red dress and the
television, and…"

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:42.430
The stunning visuals and harrowing story grab
hold of you and don't let go for 101 excruciating

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:42.940
minutes.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:52.240
It's a mind-bender of a film that will leave
you depressed for days and seriously contemplating

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.280
your own bad habits.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:55.739
Dogville

00:00:55.739 --> 00:01:00.290
Lars Von Trier's movies all tend to fit in
the category of just-one-timers, and that's

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:04.659
certainly the case with the second installment
of his Land of Opportunity trilogy, Dogville.

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:09.021
This film, which is told in nine parts, chronicles
the grueling experience of a woman who, on

00:01:09.021 --> 00:01:12.600
the run from the mafia, decides to hide out
in a rural town.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.610
The residents agree to provide her shelter
in exchange for her completing their various

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.920
low-level chores.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.390
But her relationship with the town descends
into unethical abuses of the woman, including

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:27.090
multiple sexual assaults — all very difficult
to stomach the first time around, let alone

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.110
on a repeat viewing.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.850
Passion of the Christ

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:35.040
Director Mel Gibson threw everything he had
into this harrowing, vicious film about the

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.020
final days of Jesus Christ.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.259
As the title indicates, it was meant to be
a showcase of just how much grace and conviction

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:45.509
the religious figure showed in the face of
extreme brutality from his executioners.

00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:49.770
And while the film certainly did its job of
reminding its intended audience of the sacrifices

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:54.240
Jesus endured, even viewers who might've revered
its message the first time may have a hard

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.270
time bearing witness to the shocking level
of violence again.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.799
Bad Lieutenant

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:04.369
Onscreen sexual violence can be upsetting
in virtually any form, but the sheer lechery

00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:08.450
of the central character in Bad Lieutenant
is especially difficult to observe.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.709
The movie serves to ultimately exonerate the
drug-addled, unpredictable officer.

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:16.790
But the journey to get to that point teems
with tough material that includes the assault

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:21.510
of a nun and his use of authority to force
two young women into a sexual act.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.430
It's impossible to unsee the events of the
movie — and hard to imagine voluntarily

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.320
watching them again.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.320
Boys Don't Cry

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.319
Hilary Swank's Academy Award-worthy performance
as Brandon Teena, a real-life transgender

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:38.330
man who was raped and murdered in 1993, was
certainly groundbreaking, as it shed light

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:42.180
on an underexplored sector of hate crimes
that were — and are still — being committed

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.819
against transgender individuals.

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:48.810
At the same time, it was also incredibly difficult
to bear and probably counts for many as a

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.989
movie that's worth watching once … but maybe
just the one time.

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:54.830
Irreversible

00:02:54.830 --> 00:03:00.290
This 2002 French horror film tops many best
and worst lists precisely because of how horrific

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.930
its lengthy and gratuitous rape scene is.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.891
The film tells the reverse chronological story
of a woman who is raped and beaten into a

00:03:06.891 --> 00:03:08.959
coma by a notorious criminal.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:12.860
And it includes several scenes of intense
cruelty and graphic violence that makes it

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.720
almost unwatchable the first time around for
any audience members with functional eyes.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.319
We Need to Talk About Kevin

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:23.250
As prevalent as mass murder attacks might
be in real life, it's still quite hard to

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:28.239
enjoy any piece of artwork that tries to synthesize
the psyche and history of a young person who

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:29.920
commits these heinous acts.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.140
In the case of We Need to Talk About Kevin,
an all-star cast delivers sound, impactful

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.910
work to peel back the layers of life that
led up to the title character's horrific actions.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.010
"You did not."

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:44.860
That said, the movie is still difficult to
process and might leave viewers feeling unfulfilled

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.590
and even more hopeless and confused than before.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.879
Maybe that's the whole point — to highlight
the sheer senselessness of it all — but

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:55.010
it's still hard to imagine working up an appetite
to watch it again.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:56.010
Precious

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:01.329
Based on the autobiographical novel Push by
Sapphire, Precious made for incredible, indelible

00:04:01.329 --> 00:04:06.349
cinema thanks to some blistering truth about
a segment of society most people might not

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:07.349
understand.

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:11.290
The movie tells the story of a young girl
whose life consists of parental abuse and

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:16.310
neglect first, poverty second, and whose struggle
to survive the mean streets and her even meaner

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.260
home life is a daily battle.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.410
Anyone who's seen it no doubt walked away
a little different than they came in … if

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.120
unwilling to watch it again.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.040
"You ain't gonna see me no more."

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.620
Blue Valentine

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:33.930
Derek Cianfrance's Blue Valentine brims with
raw emotion and sexuality, but it's the depressing

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.710
nature of the narrative that really resorts
this one to the dustiest shelves.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.340
The film is a categorical showcase of rich
acting by its leads, Michelle Williams and

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.340
Ryan Gosling.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:48.060
But the biting, bitter truth that even the
most earnest romances can sputter out so spectacularly

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.950
might hit a little too close to home, in a
very literal sense, for many viewers to push

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:52.340
play again.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.700
Schindler's List

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:58.520
There's no doubt Schindler's List is one of
the most important films to ever grace a theater.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.621
Steven Spielberg's adaptation of this work
of historical fiction gave light to the real

00:05:02.621 --> 00:05:07.220
work Oskar Schindler did to save the lives
of his Jewish employees during World War II.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:11.200
It painfully depicted the real horrors of
the era in unforgiving detail.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.220
In fact, it's precisely because of the raw
sense of dread and devastation that it ranks

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:19.400
among those that must be seen at least once
— but is probably difficult to revisit for

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:20.780
most.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.640
American History X

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:27.030
Tony Kaye's American History X went to some
incredibly disturbing lengths to capture the

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:31.050
true depravity that neo-Naziism inspires and
is fueled by.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:35.050
And while the overarching narrative is optimistic
in the sense that empathy is within reach

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:39.590
for even the most seemingly unsalvageable
individuals, the road to that character recovery

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:41.590
is immensely upsetting to watch.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:45.860
Not only are the language and imagery purposefully
offensive, but the acts of violence committed

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.720
in the movie are unspeakable.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:51.710
It's hard to imagine anyone choosing to endure
it all again after seeing it the first time.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:52.710
Thanks for watching!

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.570
